#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153338 :tutorial/content "How does a proportional relationship look when we plot it in the $(x,y)$ plane?\n\nTo answer this, let's consider the following proportional relationship:\n\n$$\ny = 2x\n$$\n\nTo plot a graph, we first need a table of values. \n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |\n| $1$ |  |\n| $2$ |  |\n| $3$ |  |\n\nFilling in the $y$-values using the equation $y = 2x,$ we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $3$ | $6$ |\n\nSo, we have the following $(x,y)$ coordinates:\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\quad (1,2),  \\quad (2,4),  \\quad (3,6)\n$$\n\nIf we plot these points in the coordinate plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/a8/a8cd951c66c67b8f28b791011c7553675b3112029d9ebe4b19769127b5e75bd5.png)\n\nThis leads us to the following important fact:\n\n*The graph of any proportional relationship is a straight line that passes through the origin $(0,0)$.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "becbb429-3b12-59c4-8172-aef250c47df9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1507 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153339 :tutorial/content "When there is a proportional relationship between $x$ and $y,$ we can express this using the **proportionality symbol** $\\propto,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny \\propto x\n$$\n\nNote that the following statements are all equivalent:\n\n- $y = kx$\n\n- $y$ **varies directly** with $x$\n\n- $y$ is **directly proportional** to $x$\n\n- $y \\propto x$" :tutorial/id #uuid "503f0382-0b82-583d-898a-bc1dd1aa02d1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1508 :tutorial/title "Alternative Notations and Terminology"}]}
